        "claims_meta": [
          {
            "claim_text": "“Meta had run a test that produced exactly this kind of surprise.”",
            "origin": "translation",
            "related_fact_id": "MUSE-HC-006",
            "local_context": "Ring, ring... A call came from an AI agent. That was what it seemed. But while the conversation continued, the voice on the other end was not AI. It was a person.\n\nMeta had run a test that produced exactly this kind of surprise.\n\nThe setting was the phone feature of Muse, an AI agent. In some calls made through Muse, trained contract workers, not AI, made the calls and carried the conversations through to the end.",
            "fallback_used": false
          },
          {
            "claim_text": "“A person can take over when AI alone has trouble.”",
            "origin": "ja_source",
            "related_fact_id": "MUSE-HC-002",
            "local_context": "The setting was the phone feature of Muse, an AI agent. In some calls made through Muse, trained contract workers, not AI, made the calls and carried the conversations through to the end.\n\nIt was like an AI costume: take it off, and a person steps out. Of course, having a person handle a call is not automatically bad. A person can take over when AI alone has trouble. As a system, that may even seem useful.\n\nBut then a problem appeared.",
            "fallback_used": false
          },
          {
            "claim_text": "“People feel differently when they think they are speaking to a machine and when they know a person is listening. Names, plans, and private matters are easier to share when you know who is hearing them.”",
            "origin": "ja_source",
            "related_fact_id": "MUSE-HC-010",
            "local_context": "### What Callers Were Not Told\n\nWhen contract workers handled calls, sensitive user information could be shared with call-center staff. People feel differently when they think they are speaking to a machine and when they know a person is listening. Names, plans, and private matters are easier to share when you know who is hearing them. The test began without explaining that contract staff might call.\n\nThis was where the story changed.",
            "fallback_used": false
          },
          {
            "claim_text": "“As AI makes calls and reservations, useful features make people want to know whether AI or a person is on the other end.”",
            "origin": "ja_source",
            "related_fact_id": "MUSE-HC-004",
            "local_context": "### The Reveal Behind the AI Sign\n\nThe reveal was not AI learning to sound human. A human was behind the AI sign. As AI makes calls and reservations, useful features make people want to know whether AI or a person is on the other end. A Meta executive called the undisclosed test a “mistake,” and the “human concierge” feature was rolled back for now.\n\n## In one line",
            "fallback_used": false
          }
        ]
      },
